@meta
id: 25-14
title: A macro over a marked range
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: ex-normal, range-syntax
par: 33
minutes: 7
vscode: five careful multi-cursor edits, then the same again for the next block
open: customers.fixture.ts
vimrc: let @q="0f;s', name: '\<Esc>f;s', country: '\<Esc>0i  { id: '\<Esc>A' },\<Esc>"

@brief
Register `q` already holds a macro. Played on a semicolon-separated line
it produces the object literal the fixture wants:

    cus_501;Ada Lovelace;GB   →     { id: 'cus_501', name: 'Ada Lovelace', country: 'GB' },

Run it over the five rows of the `customers` array. The two rows in
`archived` below are staying as they are — that array is fed to a
different loader and the raw form is deliberate.

`@q` is a normal-mode command like any other, so `:normal @q` plays it on
every line of a range. Mark the two ends of the block and use the marks
as the range.

@keys
| `ma` | set mark `a` on this line |
| `:'a,'bnormal @q` | play macro `q` on every line from mark `a` to mark `b` |
| `:'<,'>normal @q` | the same over the last visual selection |

@hints
Marks were chapter 17. Put `a` on the first row and `b` on the last row
of the `customers` array, then write the range with them.
--
A mark used as an address is `'a` — apostrophe, then the letter.
--
`2Gma`, `6Gmb`, then `:'a,'bnormal @q` Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    2G ma   6G mb   :'a,'bnormal @q<Enter>   :wq<Enter>

Twenty-eight keystrokes, five rows converted, and the `archived` rows
untouched.

`5@q` from the first row would have done the same work. Two reasons to
prefer this shape. First, `5@q` needs you to count the rows and to trust
that the macro lands on the next line every time — a macro that ends
somewhere unexpected takes the remaining plays with it. `:normal` puts
the cursor at the start of each line in the range itself, so a macro that
wanders cannot run off into the rest of the file. Second, a count that is
one too high eats a line of the next array; a range cannot, because you
said where it ends.

Marks rather than line numbers because you set them while you were
looking at the block. `2G` and `6G` here were only to get there; in a
real file you search to the top row, `ma`, search to the last, `mb`, and
the range is `'a,'b` without a number anywhere. The marks also move with
the lines if something above them changes.

Ranges take every other kind of mark too: `'<` and `'>` are the ends of
the last visual selection, which is what chapter 22's `:'<,'>` was all
along, and `` `` `` is where you jumped from.

@start customers.fixture.ts
export const customers = [
cus_501;Ada Lovelace;GB
cus_502;Grace Hopper;US
cus_503;Alan Turing;GB
cus_504;Edsger Dijkstra;NL
cus_505;Barbara Liskov;US
];

export const archived = [
cus_601;Ken Thompson;US
cus_602;Dennis Ritchie;US
];

@expected customers.fixture.ts
export const customers = [
  { id: 'cus_501', name: 'Ada Lovelace', country: 'GB' },
  { id: 'cus_502', name: 'Grace Hopper', country: 'US' },
  { id: 'cus_503', name: 'Alan Turing', country: 'GB' },
  { id: 'cus_504', name: 'Edsger Dijkstra', country: 'NL' },
  { id: 'cus_505', name: 'Barbara Liskov', country: 'US' },
];

export const archived = [
cus_601;Ken Thompson;US
cus_602;Dennis Ritchie;US
];
